{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-107131" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${0,1,t}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{2}[t]$\n- $2-t$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${0,1,t}$\n- ${0,1,t}$ is a basis of ${R}_{2}[t]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-107131" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1+0\\cdot t=0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n2-t=0\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot t.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-107131" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c57ba67cd0eb5a067c4072ae0da0fa8fedfdad81a1d1cf2daa6edef3409d7984"} {"question" "q-107131" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-107131" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-107131" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${0,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{3}[t]$\n- $3{t}^{2}-t$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${0,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}}$\n- ${0,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}_{3}[t]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot t+0\\cdot {t}^{2}+0\\cdot {t}^{3}=0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n3{t}^{2}-t=0\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot t+3\\cdot {t}^{2}+0\\cdot {t}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c57ba67cd0eb5a067c4072ae0da0fa8fedfdad81a1d1cf2daa6edef3409d7984"} {"question" "q-94847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${0,{t}^{3}}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{3}[t]$\n- $-{t}^{3}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${0,{t}^{3}}$\n- ${0,{t}^{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}_{3}[t]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot {t}^{3}=0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n-{t}^{3}=0\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot {t}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c57ba67cd0eb5a067c4072ae0da0fa8fedfdad81a1d1cf2daa6edef3409d7984"} {"question" "q-94850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${t,{t}^{2}}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{2}[t]$\n- ${t}^{2}+1$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${t,{t}^{2}}$\n- ${t,{t}^{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}_{2}[t]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot t+{x}_{2}\\cdot {t}^{2}=0,\n$$\n\n \nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that all linear combinations of\n\n$$\n{t,{t}^{2}}\n$$\n\nresult in polynomials without the constant term.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, the polynomial ${t}^{2}+1$ can't be written as a linear combination of the set  ${t,{t}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c57ba67cd0eb5a067c4072ae0da0fa8fedfdad81a1d1cf2daa6edef3409d7984"} {"question" "q-94842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94848" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${1,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{3}[t]$\n- ${t}^{3}-3t+8$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${1,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}}$\n- ${1,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}_{3}[t]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94848" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot 1+{x}_{2}\\cdot t+{x}_{3}\\cdot {t}^{2}+{x}_{4}\\cdot {t}^{3}=0,\n$$\n\n \nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-3t+8=8\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot t+0\\cdot {t}^{2}+1\\cdot {t}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the set is linearly independent and for any polynomial in ${R}_{3}[t],$ we get \n\n$$\na{t}^{3}+b{t}^{2}+ct+d=d\\cdot 1+c\\cdot t+b\\cdot {t}^{2}+a\\cdot {t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94848" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c57ba67cd0eb5a067c4072ae0da0fa8fedfdad81a1d1cf2daa6edef3409d7984"} {"question" "q-94848" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94848" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94848" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]=3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{11}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ a & 2b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c57ba67cd0eb5a067c4072ae0da0fa8fedfdad81a1d1cf2daa6edef3409d7984"} {"question" "q-94862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{12}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c57ba67cd0eb5a067c4072ae0da0fa8fedfdad81a1d1cf2daa6edef3409d7984"} {"question" "q-94859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-107534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-107534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{21}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & -a \\\\ 0 & 2b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-107534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c57ba67cd0eb5a067c4072ae0da0fa8fedfdad81a1d1cf2daa6edef3409d7984"} {"question" "q-107534" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-107534" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-107534" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]=(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{22}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & 6b \\\\ -2c & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c57ba67cd0eb5a067c4072ae0da0fa8fedfdad81a1d1cf2daa6edef3409d7984"} {"question" "q-94868" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94868" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94868" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the coefficients are nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]=0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c57ba67cd0eb5a067c4072ae0da0fa8fedfdad81a1d1cf2daa6edef3409d7984"} {"question" "q-94864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94943" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of a vector space $V.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of $V$\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},w}$ is linearly dependent\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{3}}$ is linearly independent" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94943" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $B$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false since the vector ${b}_{3}$ can't be written as a linear combination of ${{b}_{1},{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}}.$ But then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & ={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}({b}_{1}-{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){b}_{1}+(-{x}_{2}){b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $B$ is linearly dependent. This would contradict the fact that $B$ is a basis.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $B$ is a basis of $V,$ it is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors. This means that if we add any new vector to the set, it becomes linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, if ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent then there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere at least one of $x$'s is not zero. But then \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+0{b}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $B$ is linearly dependent. This would contradict the fact that $B$ is a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94943" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c57ba67cd0eb5a067c4072ae0da0fa8fedfdad81a1d1cf2daa6edef3409d7984"} {"question" "q-94943" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94943" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-94943" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-34638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- ${{c}_{3},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-34638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$, any vector of ${R}^{2},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}.$ This means that ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement III is true. The set ${{c}_{3},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because, by reordering the vectors, we obtain the basis $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-34638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c57ba67cd0eb5a067c4072ae0da0fa8fedfdad81a1d1cf2daa6edef3409d7984"} {"question" "q-34638" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-34638" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-34638" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-107589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be bases of ${R}^{3}$ and ${R}^{2}$, respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-107589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$, any vector of ${R}^{2},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the vector ${b}_{3}$ is in ${R}^{3}$ but it is not a linear combination of  ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ as the set  ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. This means that the set does not span ${R}^{3}$ and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement III is false. The set ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-107589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c57ba67cd0eb5a067c4072ae0da0fa8fedfdad81a1d1cf2daa6edef3409d7984"} {"question" "q-107589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-107589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-107589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of the statements are true"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-34768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be bases of ${R}^{3}$ and ${R}^{2}$, respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- ${{b}_{3},{b}_{1},{b}_{1}+{b}_{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- ${x,{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-34768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{3},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$, any vector of ${R}^{2},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set ${{b}_{3},{b}_{1},{b}_{1}+{b}_{3}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the third vector is a linear combination of ${b}_{3}$ and ${b}_{1}.$ This means that the set is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement III is false. The set ${x,{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-34768" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c57ba67cd0eb5a067c4072ae0da0fa8fedfdad81a1d1cf2daa6edef3409d7984"} {"question" "q-34768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-34768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-34768" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of the statements are true"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-107588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- ${{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},2{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-107588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$, any vector of ${R}^{3},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This means that ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}({b}_{1})+{x}_{2}(2{b}_{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+(2{x}_{2}){b}_{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}}$ spans ${R}^{2}$ since any linear combination of $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2}(2{b}_{2})\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The set ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},2{c}_{2}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the third vector is a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that the set is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-107588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c57ba67cd0eb5a067c4072ae0da0fa8fedfdad81a1d1cf2daa6edef3409d7984"} {"question" "q-107588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-107588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "q-107588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/state.json" "file_sha256" "d10e7913d7c70c30eef5b4bd5fd200dfe7c5dc070011d70ac56c89daa175ceb5"} {"question" "e-8269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\big\\{ 0,t^2 \\big\\}$ is a linearly independent set in $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$\n\n- $-5t^2$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\big\\{ 0, t^2 \\big\\}$\n\n- $\\big\\{ 0, t^2 \\big\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/example-8269.json" "file_sha256" "15d0ccdb9baabe9a28183baab4b331934598c49c43e209926c9abb047884d18e"} {"question" "e-8269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1 \\cdot {\\color{blue}0} + 0 \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2} = 0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n-5t^2 = 0 \\cdot {\\color{blue}0} + (-5) \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/example-8269.json" "file_sha256" "15d0ccdb9baabe9a28183baab4b331934598c49c43e209926c9abb047884d18e"} {"question" "e-4830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector space $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/assets/f80056f73ec3b3e73a1279990bc8131e6a712af3f62865384a6bd6f6f35595d1.png) is a linearly independent set in $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$\n\n- $[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/assets/d56c111afc375f1cecb7088aba7c67009b5087430e20b84811f9ad45b947df70.png)\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/assets/f0bf564ae35e7aa8bb743034bb915a46ea0ec753ccfd06fb45204201d9b1fa3d.png) is a basis of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/example-4830.json" "file_sha256" "1813b944a62ec907c81f3928ef26ba53555d4d91dacd6f4f6ab93f7b8dbe79dc"} {"question" "e-4830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/assets/8c5e7ba97572e7e0a0c548f8877a609e85096c646854bc4c3d2a693943b20496.png)\nis true if and only if $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/assets/4688890d66d47e92b0e38e603e1b885125dc143a4582b11db92acbf5cdc74ab1.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/assets/697c21a322d1136432b9ac23f959e766f972b829d790d88dc6be4163dce83669.png)\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry $a_{11} = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/assets/0ba338d733e2510ece4a75c2f16acfd1e79f7edbce46232dba8480ab7f37a666.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/example-4830.json" "file_sha256" "1813b944a62ec907c81f3928ef26ba53555d4d91dacd6f4f6ab93f7b8dbe79dc"} {"question" "e-4831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ be a basis of a vector space $V.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2},- \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$ is a basis of $V$\n\n- $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0}  \\right\\}$ spans $V$\n\n- $\\left\\{  -\\mathbf{b_2}, 3\\mathbf{b_1}\\right\\}$ is linearly dependent" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/example-4831.json" "file_sha256" "1005eb6fba1cce9b4e042e65e0e2f78e55fa009f8e1b5e994064562f724fabd4"} {"question" "e-4831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $\\mathcal{B}$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{2}+{x}_{2}(-{b}_{1})+{x}_{3}({b}_{3}+{b}_{1}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{2}+({x}_{3}-{x}_{2}){b}_{1}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/assets/44e05962671a66862ed2fb7a4bc3fc2f859d3a66c56274b869724ee47f6c6414.png)\nAlso, $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2},- \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $\\mathcal{B}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2},- \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{b}_1+x_2\\mathbf{b}_2+x_3\\mathbf{b}_3\n=\nx_2\\mathbf{b}_2+(x_3-x_1)(-\\mathbf{b}_1)+x_3 (\\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1})\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. In order for $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0}  \\right\\}$ to span $V,$ every element of $\\mathcal{B}$ must be able to be expressed as a linear combination of elements of $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0}  \\right\\}.$ But the vector $\\mathbf{b}_1 \\in \\mathcal{B}$ can't be written as a linear combination of the elements of $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0}  \\right\\}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If $\\left\\{  -\\mathbf{b_2}, 3\\mathbf{b_1}\\right\\}$ is linearly dependent then there exist $x_1$ and $x_2$ not both zero such that\n\n$$\nx_1(-\\mathbf{b_2}) + x_2(3\\mathbf{b_1}) = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nThen, we would have\n\n$$\n(3x_2) \\cdot \\mathbf{b_1} + (-x_1) \\cdot \\mathbf{b_2} + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{b}_3= \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nBut this would mean that $\\mathcal{B} = \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent, which contradicts the fact that $\\mathcal{B}$ is a basis of $V.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066905/example-4831.json" "file_sha256" "1005eb6fba1cce9b4e042e65e0e2f78e55fa009f8e1b5e994064562f724fabd4"}] "usage" {"e-8269" [] "e-4830" [] "q-34638" [] "e-4831" []} "basis" 2216 "retractions" [[17592186124772 :question/problem "Let $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$ and $C = {c_{1}, c_{2}, c_{3}}$ be bases of $R^{2}$ and $R^{3}$, respectively.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. ${b_{1}, b_{2}, x}$ is a basis of $R^{2}$\n2. ${b_{1}, b_{2}}$ is a basis of $R^{3}$\n3. ${c_{3}, c_{2}, c_{1}}$ is a basis of $R^{3}$"] [17592186124321 :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb R$ with degrees at most $2$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\big\\{0,t^2 \\big\\}$ is a linearly independent set in $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$\n2. $-5t^2$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\big\\{0, t^2 \\big\\}$\n3. $\\big\\{0, t^2 \\big\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$"] [17592186124321 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n$1 \\cdot {\\color{blue}0} + 0 \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2} = 0$,\nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n$-5t^2 = 0 \\cdot {\\color{blue}0} + (-5) \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2}$.\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186124217 :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is a linearly independent set in $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$\n2. $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is a basis of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$"] [17592186124217 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nis true if and only if $x_1=x_2=0$. So, the set is linearly independent.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ can't be written as a linear combination of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry $a_{11} = 0$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186124218 :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ be a basis of a vector space $V$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2},-\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$ is a basis of $V$\n2. $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$ spans $V$\n3. $\\left\\{-\\mathbf{b_2}, 3\\mathbf{b_1}\\right\\}$ is linearly dependent"] [17592186124218 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathcal{B}$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V$, and\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ spans $V$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n$x_{1}b_{2} + x_{2}(-b_{1}) + x_{3}(b_{3} + b_{1})|= 0$\nis equivalent to\n$x_{1}b_{2} + (x_{3} - x_{2})b_{1} + x_{3}b_{3}|= 0$,\nwhich, since $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n${x_{1} = 0; x_{3} - x_{2} = 0; x_{3} = 0$\nAlso, $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2},-\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $\\mathcal{B}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2},-\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$:\n$x_1\\mathbf{b}_1+x_2\\mathbf{b}_2+x_3\\mathbf{b}_3 = x_2\\mathbf{b}_2+(x_3-x_1)(-\\mathbf{b}_1)+x_3 (\\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1})$\n- Statement II is false. In order for $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$ to span $V$, every element of $\\mathcal{B}$ must be able to be expressed as a linear combination of elements of $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$. But the vector $\\mathbf{b}_1 \\in \\mathcal{B}$ can't be written as a linear combination of the elements of $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$.\n- Statement III is false. If $\\left\\{-\\mathbf{b_2}, 3\\mathbf{b_1}\\right\\}$ is linearly dependent then there exist $x_1$ and $x_2$ not both zero such that\n$x_1(-\\mathbf{b_2}) + x_2(3\\mathbf{b_1}) = \\mathbf{0}$.\nThen, we would have\n$(3x_2) \\cdot \\mathbf{b_1} + (-x_1) \\cdot \\mathbf{b_2} + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{b}_3= \\mathbf{0}$.\nBut this would mean that $\\mathcal{B} = \\left\\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent, which contradicts the fact that $\\mathcal{B}$ is a basis of $V$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186121655 17592186121656 17592186121657 17592186121658 17592186121659] "immutable_fields" [[17592186121654 :answer-field/correct] [17592186121654 :answer-field/id] [17592186121654 :answer-field/key] [17592186121654 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
